are not that important now.

00:18:49.880 --> 00:18:52.010
And as you can see,
this is a lot of data.

00:18:52.010 --> 00:18:57.400
So put it in a database that
gives me index of result

00:18:57.400 --> 00:18:59.360
is a good way to serve this.

00:18:59.360 --> 00:19:01.750
MINHAZ KAZI: So what is this
trend line on the left side?

00:19:01.750 --> 00:19:02.680
FELIPE HOFFA: Oh yeah,
a couple of tricks

00:19:02.680 --> 00:19:03.700
when using Data Studio.

00:19:03.700 --> 00:19:06.310
In this case, I managed
to get a [INAUDIBLE]..

00:19:06.310 --> 00:19:08.380
You can change the tag
for anything else-- let's

00:19:08.380 --> 00:19:09.160
say, TensorFlow.

00:19:09.160 --> 00:19:10.160
MINHAZ KAZI: TensorFlow?

00:19:10.160 --> 00:19:12.130
All right.

00:19:12.130 --> 00:19:13.420
FELIPE HOFFA: Or JavaScript.

00:19:13.420 --> 00:19:16.073
Let's see if JavaScript is
trending up or down on--

00:19:16.073 --> 00:19:17.730
MINHAZ KAZI: Let's
do JavaScript.

00:19:17.730 --> 00:19:18.855
FELIPE HOFFA: OK, anything.

00:19:18.855 --> 00:19:20.550
We can just jump anywhere here.

00:19:20.550 --> 00:19:23.650
And you can see that JavaScript
is not getting any more views,

00:19:23.650 --> 00:19:26.060
but you can get this
little thread here.

00:19:26.060 --> 00:19:28.959
And you can also see
those little lines there

00:19:28.959 --> 00:19:29.500
in each line.

00:19:29.500 --> 00:19:31.490
MINHAZ KAZI: How
do you do those?

00:19:31.490 --> 00:19:37.680
FELIPE HOFFA: Oh, those we build
using just some SQL magic to--

00:19:37.680 --> 00:19:40.340
and if you want to
see how we made them,

00:19:40.340 --> 00:19:43.630
we did this as a collaborative
project on Stack Overflow.

00:19:43.630 --> 00:19:46.330
So we have-- looked for
[INAUDIBLE] in Big Query,

00:19:46.330 --> 00:19:50.580
you will find my answer and
[INAUDIBLE] and Mikhail's.

00:19:50.580 --> 00:19:51.280
We got there.

00:19:51.280 --> 00:19:52.660
MINHAZ KAZI: They don't
want to know about SQL.

00:19:52.660 --> 00:19:54.520
They just want to
drag and drop and make

00:19:54.520 --> 00:19:55.561
beautiful visualizations.

00:19:55.561 --> 00:19:58.360
FELIPE HOFFA: Yeah, but this is
a pretty cool trend line here.

00:19:58.360 --> 00:20:01.397
MINHAZ KAZI: So can you tell us
what you exactly did behind us?

00:20:01.397 --> 00:20:02.980
How did you set up
your data pipeline?

00:20:02.980 --> 00:20:03.896
FELIPE HOFFA: Oh, yes.

00:20:03.896 --> 00:20:06.070
Let's go back to how we
made this with CloudSQL.

00:20:06.070 --> 00:20:07.926
Basically, I had Big Query.

00:20:07.926 --> 00:20:11.350
And in Big Query ran my huge
analysis reducing the data.

00:20:11.350 --> 00:20:13.180
I store it in cloud storage.

00:20:13.180 --> 00:20:15.340
That can be imported
into CloudSQL,

00:20:15.340 --> 00:20:17.940
which is MySQL in this case.

00:20:17.940 --> 00:20:20.811
And then Data
Studio can read it.

00:20:20.811 --> 00:20:22.810
MINHAZ KAZI: OK, so you
have this whole pipeline

00:20:22.810 --> 00:20:24.944
where you are getting
data from Big Query,

00:20:24.944 --> 00:20:26.110
putting it in cloud storage.

00:20:26.110 --> 00:20:29.020
And from cloud storage, it's
getting imported into CloudSQL.

00:20:29.020 --> 00:20:32.207
And Data Studio just uses its
native connector to CloudSQL

00:20:32.207 --> 00:20:33.040
to pull in the data.

00:20:33.040 --> 00:20:35.906
And the data is fast
because it's indexed.

00:20:35.906 --> 00:20:38.030
And data studio has a native
connector to CloudSQL.

00:20:38.030 --> 00:20:39.490
You don't have to set
up the firewall rules.

00:20:39.490 --> 00:20:40.699
It just works out of the box.

00:20:40.699 --> 00:20:41.614
FELIPE HOFFA: Exactly.

00:20:41.614 --> 00:20:42.600
So it's pretty easy.

00:20:42.600 --> 00:20:45.370
And then I could put any
other catch in the middle.

00:20:45.370 --> 00:20:48.710
CloudSQL was just pretty
easy for me for this case.

00:20:48.710 --> 00:20:50.470
MINHAZ KAZI: OK.

00:20:50.470 --> 00:20:52.330
FELIPE HOFFA: What
about private data?

00:20:52.330 --> 00:20:55.480
Because all of these, we've
been looking at public datasets.

00:20:55.480 --> 00:20:59.800
But I want to bring these
principles also to my own data.

00:20:59.800 --> 00:21:00.550
MINHAZ KAZI: Sure.

00:21:00.550 --> 00:21:01.390
We can do that.

00:21:01.390 --> 00:21:04.090
Like, we looked at Stack
Exchange data, Wikipedia data.

00:21:04.090 --> 00:21:07.030
But you might have private
data where you want

00:21:07.030 --> 00:21:09.340
to use the data with yourself.

00:21:09.340 --> 00:21:11.226
Or maybe it's an
organizational data

00:21:11.226 --> 00:21:13.600
where do you want multiple
people within the organization

00:21:13.600 --> 00:21:14.990
to look at the same data.

00:21:14.990 --> 00:21:17.500
From there, the question
also becomes that, hey,

00:21:17.500 --> 00:21:19.210
can I use templates?

00:21:19.210 --> 00:21:21.040
I have my private
data and I want

00:21:21.040 --> 00:21:23.650
to create a template where
all my internal users can use

00:21:23.650 --> 00:21:25.540
the same template with my data.

00:21:25.540 --> 00:21:28.420
Or can I create a template
where different private users

00:21:28.420 --> 00:21:30.620
can use the same
template with their data?

00:21:30.620 --> 00:21:32.210
And the thing is,
yes, it is possible.

00:21:32.210 --> 00:21:35.439
Data Studio lets you use all
the reports as templates.

00:21:35.439 --> 00:21:36.730
And we have a Template Gallery.

00:21:36.730 --> 00:21:39.320
If you go to
datastudio.google.com/gallery,

00:21:39.320 --> 00:21:42.580
there's a huge gallery with all
different kinds of templates

00:21:42.580 --> 00:21:43.090
available.

00:21:43.090 --> 00:21:45.356
And we will look at one example.

00:21:45.356 --> 00:21:49.360
So this is the example for our
Google Analytics for Firebase.

00:21:49.360 --> 00:21:52.360
Let's say you have
your Google Analytics

00:21:52.360 --> 00:21:54.527
for Firebase data in Big Query.

00:21:54.527 --> 00:21:55.610
FELIPE HOFFA: [INAUDIBLE].

00:21:55.610 --> 00:21:57.250
So this is a sample
visualization

00:21:57.250 --> 00:21:58.969
with someone else's data.

00:21:58.969 --> 00:22:00.760
MINHAZ KAZI: Yes, this
is like sample data.

00:22:00.760 --> 00:22:02.830
And you have your own
dataset in Big Query.

00:22:02.830 --> 00:22:06.940
And you want to use that
dataset with this dashboard.

00:22:06.940 --> 00:22:10.570
So the way you do it is you'd
need the name of your project.

00:22:10.570 --> 00:22:13.510
So I will just take an
example because I don't

00:22:13.510 --> 00:22:14.719
have my own Firebase project.

00:22:14.719 --> 00:22:16.551
FELIPE HOFFA: That's
the name of the project

00:22:16.551 --> 00:22:19.020
you want to visualize, one
you have permissions to.

00:22:19.020 --> 00:22:21.430
MINHAZ KAZI: I'll go back
and all I have to do is use--

00:22:21.430 --> 00:22:21.960
click--

00:22:21.960 --> 00:22:23.650
click on this Use Template.

00:22:23.650 --> 00:22:28.000
And I will change and
create a new source.

00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:31.100
I know the data is
in Big Query and I

00:22:31.100 --> 00:22:32.614
know it's a shared project.

00:22:32.614 --> 00:22:34.280
If you're doing it
for your own project,

00:22:34.280 --> 00:22:36.260
you might have to go
through your My Projects

00:22:36.260 --> 00:22:37.950
and go through that flow.

00:22:37.950 --> 00:22:43.350
And I'll pick a project,
the shared project name.

00:22:43.350 --> 00:22:47.009
The data set is Analytics
and the table is Events.

00:22:47.009 --> 00:22:47.800
FELIPE HOFFA: Boom.

00:22:47.800 --> 00:22:51.130
MINHAZ KAZI: Click on
Events and click Connect.

00:22:51.130 --> 00:22:52.934
And that should be it.

00:22:52.934 --> 00:22:53.850
FELIPE HOFFA: Exactly.

00:22:53.850 --> 00:22:56.080
You created a connector
for the pretty template.

00:22:56.080 --> 00:22:59.050
And now any template
that you look--

00:22:59.050 --> 00:23:02.510
use data that you might
have the similar shape,

00:23:02.510 --> 00:23:05.512
you can use it for your
own data with Data Studio.

00:23:05.512 --> 00:23:06.220
MINHAZ KAZI: Yes.

00:23:06.220 --> 00:23:10.040
So this is the sample data
set we have on Big Query.

00:23:10.040 --> 00:23:11.440
It's using that sample data set.

00:23:11.440 --> 00:23:13.606
But I just connected that
data set to this template.

00:23:13.606 --> 00:23:14.315
And it's working.

00:23:14.315 --> 00:23:15.981
FELIPE HOFFA: Cool,
and this is working.

00:23:15.981 --> 00:23:17.950
And it's connecting
straight to Big Query.

00:23:17.950 --> 00:23:19.380
And now we're in
a similar place.

00:23:19.380 --> 00:23:21.522
Like, this is great
for a data analyst.

00:23:21.522 --> 00:23:22.230
MINHAZ KAZI: Yes.

00:23:22.230 --> 00:23:25.494
FELIPE HOFFA: But if I
want to publish this--

00:23:25.494 --> 00:23:28.760
MINHAZ KAZI: So if you
want to make this scalable,

00:23:28.760 --> 00:23:30.320
for an analyst,
what would happen

00:23:30.320 --> 00:23:33.680
is let's say analyst
would probably

00:23:33.680 --> 00:23:36.125
create a direct connection
from Big Query to Data Studio.

00:23:36.125 --> 00:23:37.000
So you write a query.

00:23:37.000 --> 00:23:39.540
You create a dashboard and you
have the direct connection.

00:23:39.540 --> 00:23:41.660
And for the analyst,
analyst this

00:23:41.660 --> 00:23:43.880
can create a dashboard
like this, for example,

00:23:43.880 --> 00:23:45.680
based on Chrome UX dataset.

00:23:45.680 --> 00:23:48.507
But they would incur
very little cost,

00:23:48.507 --> 00:23:50.340
depending on how many
queries they're doing.

00:23:50.340 --> 00:23:54.420
So if one analyst does 300
completely unique queries,

00:23:54.420 --> 00:23:56.986
so it doesn't use any of the
cache in Big Query or Data

00:23:56.986 --> 00:23:58.610
Studio, it's completely
unique queries,

00:23:58.610 --> 00:24:00.720
and on average, 30
gigabytes of query size,

00:24:00.720 --> 00:24:04.670
they will use 9 terabytes
of big data, Big Query data.

00:24:04.670 --> 00:24:06.361
And then that would
cost them $40.

00:24:06.361 --> 00:24:08.360
FELIPE HOFFA: Yeah, for
an analyst, pretty cool.

00:24:08.360 --> 00:24:12.650
Now you are querying 300
times, 30 gigabytes of data.

00:24:12.650 --> 00:24:15.380
That might be a big table,
but the price is reasonable.

00:24:15.380 --> 00:24:16.960
Everything is working fine.

00:24:16.960 --> 00:24:19.280
But then you want
to publish this.

00:24:19.280 --> 00:24:22.110
MINHAZ KAZI: That's where you
might face some difficulties,

00:24:22.110 --> 00:24:24.410
but-- because let's say you
take the same dashboard.

00:24:24.410 --> 00:24:25.790
You put it on a
website, embed it

00:24:25.790 --> 00:24:27.357
somewhere in your Medium post.

00:24:27.357 --> 00:24:29.690
And then suddenly, hundreds
of people are looking at it.

00:24:29.690 --> 00:24:31.970
So lets just assume,
hypothetical scenario,

00:24:31.970 --> 00:24:35.300
500 visitors are
coming to your website,

00:24:35.300 --> 00:24:37.730
There are four different
queries in your dashboard

00:24:37.730 --> 00:24:40.360
and each user is looking at
the dashboard six times--

00:24:40.360 --> 00:24:43.100
again, around 30
gigabytes of size.

00:24:43.100 --> 00:24:46.160
You end out with 350 terabytes
of data usage in Big Query.

00:24:46.160 --> 00:24:47.660
FELIPE HOFFA: So
the first thing you

00:24:47.660 --> 00:24:50.835
have to do when you start
playing with this is--

00:24:50.835 --> 00:24:53.210
MINHAZ KAZI: You have to have
some kind of caching there.

00:24:53.210 --> 00:24:54.737
FELIPE HOFFA: Yes, or turn on?

00:24:54.737 --> 00:24:56.570
MINHAZ KAZI: Well, yes,
that's a good point.

00:24:56.570 --> 00:24:58.635
First line of defense is this.

00:24:58.635 --> 00:25:01.010
FELIPE HOFFA: Yes turn on your
cost controls in Big Query

00:25:01.010 --> 00:25:03.350
so you will not wake
up to any surprise.

00:25:03.350 --> 00:25:04.940
These are pretty easy to use.

00:25:04.940 --> 00:25:08.570
You just turn them
on and everything--

00:25:08.570 --> 00:25:10.070
you will not wake
up to any surprise

00:25:10.070 --> 00:25:11.880
because your queries
will be contained.

00:25:11.880 --> 00:25:15.500
But them I still want to
put this dashboard online

00:25:15.500 --> 00:25:19.310
and I want to have all
my costs contained.

00:25:19.310 --> 00:25:20.950
MINHAZ KAZI: There
is a way to do it.

00:25:20.950 --> 00:25:21.060
FELIPE HOFFA: Mm-hm.

00:25:21.060 --> 00:25:22.976
MINHAZ KAZI: So what we
could do is everything

00:25:22.976 --> 00:25:25.580
that we just looked at, you
have this caching mechanism.

00:25:25.580 --> 00:25:27.800
You have templates and
you have scalability.

00:25:27.800 --> 00:25:31.170
We can put all this
together into one solution.

00:25:31.170 --> 00:25:34.950
And that is, how can your
users go from complex data

00:25:34.950 --> 00:25:37.650
to a scalable, cost-effective
dashboard in five clicks?

00:25:37.650 --> 00:25:38.934
By using community connectors.

00:25:38.934 --> 00:25:39.850
FELIPE HOFFA: Exactly.

00:25:39.850 --> 00:25:41.340
With a community
connector, we will

00:25:41.340 --> 00:25:44.130
be able to encapsulate
all of this logic

00:25:44.130 --> 00:25:47.782
without having the users need
to know all of these tricks.

00:25:47.782 --> 00:25:48.490
MINHAZ KAZI: Yes.

00:25:48.490 --> 00:25:50.781
Community connectors are,
again, written in App Script.

00:25:50.781 --> 00:25:53.280
They are connectors
for Google Data studio.

00:25:53.280 --> 00:25:54.930
You basically define
a few functions

00:25:54.930 --> 00:25:57.360
and then add your
connector to Data Studio.

00:25:57.360 --> 00:25:59.940
And then Data Studio can pull
the data through the connector.

00:25:59.940 --> 00:26:01.680
And you can implement
your own logic

00:26:01.680 --> 00:26:04.950
for caching or permissions.

00:26:04.950 --> 00:26:06.030
You can add all of that.

00:26:06.030 --> 00:26:07.530
FELIPE HOFFA: Yeah,
with App Studio,

00:26:07.530 --> 00:26:10.140
you have the flexibility to
build any logic you want.

00:26:10.140 --> 00:26:10.890
MINHAZ KAZI: Yes.

00:26:10.890 --> 00:26:14.310
For example, we took
the Chrome UX data set.

00:26:14.310 --> 00:26:16.860
From the Chrome UX data
set, you can potentially

00:26:16.860 --> 00:26:19.150
build a dashboard
that looks like this.

00:26:19.150 --> 00:26:21.670
But we wanted to give the
user more flexibility.

00:26:21.670 --> 00:26:24.030
So what we did is we
created a workflow that

00:26:24.030 --> 00:26:25.450
looks kind of like this.

00:26:25.450 --> 00:26:28.350
We have the initial
dataset in Big Query.

00:26:28.350 --> 00:26:32.250
So every month, we do an
initial level of aggregation,

00:26:32.250 --> 00:26:34.490
and we create a smaller
Big Query table.

00:26:34.490 --> 00:26:35.406
FELIPE HOFFA: Exactly.

00:26:35.406 --> 00:26:37.232
You go from the 30
gigabytes table to--

00:26:37.232 --> 00:26:38.690
MINHAZ KAZI: Less
than 2 gigabytes.

00:26:38.690 --> 00:26:40.740
FELIPE HOFFA: Less than 2
gigabytes-- that's much better.

00:26:40.740 --> 00:26:43.050
MINHAZ KAZI: Yes, so that
is our first level of cache.

00:26:43.050 --> 00:26:46.920
Then for every URL,
once per month,

00:26:46.920 --> 00:26:50.220
we cache that URL's data
into Cloud Firestore,

00:26:50.220 --> 00:26:52.500
which again has a free tier.

00:26:52.500 --> 00:26:54.930
And from Cloud Firestore,
every time someone

00:26:54.930 --> 00:26:57.430
wants to look at a dashboard
containing that URL,

00:26:57.430 --> 00:26:59.860
we pull in that data
into Apps Script Cache.

00:26:59.860 --> 00:27:03.430
Apps Script Cache
is not permanent.

00:27:03.430 --> 00:27:07.080
It will be alive maximum
from maybe a few minutes

00:27:07.080 --> 00:27:08.220
to six hours.

00:27:08.220 --> 00:27:10.854
So if you're using the connector
to connect to that data,

00:27:10.854 --> 00:27:12.270
you can get very
fast performance,

00:27:12.270 --> 00:27:13.440
but it's not persistent.

00:27:13.440 --> 00:27:15.990
So you'll have to pull
it again from Firestore.

00:27:15.990 --> 00:27:17.070
So we take all of that.

00:27:17.070 --> 00:27:20.250
We take the cache data and we
add it to a custom template.

00:27:20.250 --> 00:27:22.847
And then we have this
community connector

00:27:22.847 --> 00:27:24.180
that you can use in Data Studio.

00:27:24.180 --> 00:27:25.380
FELIPE HOFFA: Yeah,
that so Data Studio

00:27:25.380 --> 00:27:26.610
connects to the connector.

00:27:26.610 --> 00:27:28.380
You encapsulate a lot
of the logic there,

00:27:28.380 --> 00:27:31.045
and it goes on every level
of cache that you want.

00:27:31.045 --> 00:27:31.920
MINHAZ KAZI: Exactly.

00:27:31.920 --> 00:27:34.530
FELIPE HOFFA: But as a user, I
want to know my own websites.

00:27:34.530 --> 00:27:35.340
How do I use this?

00:27:35.340 --> 00:27:36.120
MINHAZ KAZI: So if
you're the user,

00:27:36.120 --> 00:27:37.090
you don't see any of this.

00:27:37.090 --> 00:27:38.839
You don't have to worry
about any of this.

00:27:38.839 --> 00:27:41.220
All you get is one URL.

00:27:41.220 --> 00:27:42.930
So you go to this URL.

00:27:42.930 --> 00:27:44.550
And we have hyperlinked it.

00:27:44.550 --> 00:27:46.300
We go to this URL.

00:27:46.300 --> 00:27:46.800
That's it.

00:27:46.800 --> 00:27:48.630
You get a new
interface where you

00:27:48.630 --> 00:27:50.770
have to add in your website.

00:27:50.770 --> 00:27:52.795
So let's try Wikipedia.

00:27:52.795 --> 00:27:55.170
FELIPE HOFFA: Wikipedia or
any of your favorite websites.

00:27:55.170 --> 00:27:57.696
We have 5 million URLs there.

00:27:57.696 --> 00:27:59.410
MINHAZ KAZI: So
we'll add Wikipedia.

00:27:59.410 --> 00:28:02.510
We will add these
origins to be modified.

00:28:02.510 --> 00:28:04.530
Connect, allow.

00:28:04.530 --> 00:28:06.280
FELIPE HOFFA: And
this is a public URL

00:28:06.280 --> 00:28:07.279
where you are right now.

00:28:07.279 --> 00:28:10.020
MINHAZ KAZI: Yes,
this is [INAUDIBLE]..

00:28:10.020 --> 00:28:12.870
And so what this is doing is
it's going into Big Query,

00:28:12.870 --> 00:28:13.810
pulling in the data.

00:28:13.810 --> 00:28:16.580
So right now, it went into
Big Query, pulled in the data,

00:28:16.580 --> 00:28:19.440
put it in Cloud Firestore,
pulled the data from Firestore,

00:28:19.440 --> 00:28:20.850
put in Apps Strip Cache.

00:28:20.850 --> 00:28:22.830
And it's now ready to
create the dashboard.

00:28:22.830 --> 00:28:24.870
So we create the
report, and we already

00:28:24.870 --> 00:28:26.970
have a template attached to it.

00:28:26.970 --> 00:28:29.190
So the user makes--

00:28:29.190 --> 00:28:31.620
puts in the URL,
makes five clicks.

00:28:31.620 --> 00:28:34.140
And they end up with their
own dashboard for the URL.

00:28:34.140 --> 00:28:34.976
FELIPE HOFFA: Ooh.

00:28:34.976 --> 00:28:36.850
MINHAZ KAZI: And this
is their own dashboard.

00:28:36.850 --> 00:28:38.760
It's not like a shared
dashboard that different people

00:28:38.760 --> 00:28:39.535
are viewing.

00:28:39.535 --> 00:28:40.410
It's their dashboard.

00:28:40.410 --> 00:28:41.530
They can go in.

00:28:41.530 --> 00:28:43.680
They can change
things, edit things.

00:28:43.680 --> 00:28:45.459
They can do whatever
basically they want.

00:28:45.459 --> 00:28:46.500
FELIPE HOFFA: [INAUDIBLE]

00:28:46.500 --> 00:28:49.300
MINHAZ KAZI: For example, I
will put in a comparison here.

00:28:49.300 --> 00:28:51.710
So I can take the same thing.

00:28:51.710 --> 00:28:58.330
I will check the URL and take
this one, copy it, paste it.

00:28:58.330 --> 00:29:02.290
So I have two things side
by side for the same URL.

00:29:02.290 --> 00:29:05.620
And maybe I will compare
with a different website.

00:29:05.620 --> 00:29:07.260
FELIPE HOFFA: Any website?

00:29:07.260 --> 00:29:09.670
MINHAZ KAZI: Any
website, anyone?

00:29:09.670 --> 00:29:11.029
FELIPE HOFFA: Any suggestion.

00:29:11.029 --> 00:29:12.070
MINHAZ KAZI: Google dot--

00:29:12.070 --> 00:29:13.319
FELIPE HOFFA: Google.com, yes.

00:29:13.319 --> 00:29:16.120
MINHAZ KAZI: [INAUDIBLE]
dot google.com.

00:29:16.120 --> 00:29:17.380
And the users can do this.

00:29:17.380 --> 00:29:19.572
We have our-- this is the
user just went into this.

00:29:19.572 --> 00:29:20.780
They can view the same thing.

00:29:20.780 --> 00:29:23.770
So you have side-by-side
comparison of your website

00:29:23.770 --> 00:29:25.580
with any other
website that you pick.

00:29:25.580 --> 00:29:28.410
And this is
real-life usage data.

00:29:28.410 --> 00:29:30.540
It's not even like sample data.

00:29:30.540 --> 00:29:34.550
This is total Chrome
anonymous usage statistics.

00:29:34.550 --> 00:29:37.050
FELIPE HOFFA: And in
this case, the connector

00:29:37.050 --> 00:29:38.652
is using your credentials.

00:29:38.652 --> 00:29:39.360
MINHAZ KAZI: Yes.

00:29:39.360 --> 00:29:41.310
FELIPE HOFFA: Like-- and user
can create the new instance

00:29:41.310 --> 00:29:42.018
of the connector.

00:29:42.018 --> 00:29:44.476
The credentials are
privately stored there?

00:29:44.476 --> 00:29:46.350
MINHAZ KAZI: Yes, the
credentials we're using

00:29:46.350 --> 00:29:47.350
is on a service account.

00:29:47.350 --> 00:29:50.640
So whoever created the
connector is bearing the cost.

00:29:50.640 --> 00:29:52.964
So the users who are
using this connector,

00:29:52.964 --> 00:29:55.380
they don't have to worry about
having a Big Query account,

00:29:55.380 --> 00:29:56.580
or they don't have
to worry about cost,

00:29:56.580 --> 00:29:58.020
because they're not
incurring any cost.

00:29:58.020 --> 00:29:59.250
FELIPE HOFFA: And you
don't worry about the cost

00:29:59.250 --> 00:30:01.380
either because you built
all of these cutting layers.

00:30:01.380 --> 00:30:03.505
MINHAZ KAZI: Yes, so we
can have a look at the cost

00:30:03.505 --> 00:30:05.680
now if we go back.

00:30:05.680 --> 00:30:08.260
Initially, we were
incurring this much cost

00:30:08.260 --> 00:30:10.940
and this much data usage
for this connector.

00:30:10.940 --> 00:30:12.682
But if we implement
the new model

00:30:12.682 --> 00:30:14.140
for caching, what
ends up happening

00:30:14.140 --> 00:30:16.570
is it's the same 500 users.

00:30:16.570 --> 00:30:19.420
But you get less than one
query for dashboard view

00:30:19.420 --> 00:30:21.250
because you have
everything cached.

00:30:21.250 --> 00:30:23.320
And your query size
becomes 2 gigabytes,

00:30:23.320 --> 00:30:27.160
so it's less than 1 terabyte
of Big Query data usage, which

00:30:27.160 --> 00:30:28.690
is in the free tier.

00:30:28.690 --> 00:30:31.180
And it's less than 10
gigabytes of Firebase usage,

00:30:31.180 --> 00:30:32.720
with is, again,
in the free tier.

00:30:32.720 --> 00:30:34.870
So your cost becomes zero.

00:30:34.870 --> 00:30:37.270
That's how you can
encapsulate all of this

00:30:37.270 --> 00:30:39.526
and create a complete solution.

00:30:39.526 --> 00:30:41.960
But what about permissions?

00:30:41.960 --> 00:30:44.440
Oh, good thing is if you
want to create new community

00:30:44.440 --> 00:30:46.600
connectors, we
have this Codelab.

00:30:46.600 --> 00:30:48.100
You can go to this
Codelab and learn

00:30:48.100 --> 00:30:50.950
how to create your own community
connector using Apps Script.

00:30:50.950 --> 00:30:53.170
It should take you about
15 minutes to 30 minutes.

00:30:53.170 --> 00:30:55.930
And you can create
connectors to any Web

00:30:55.930 --> 00:30:57.674
API or external services.

00:30:57.674 --> 00:30:59.590
FELIPE HOFFA: Yes, and
it's an amazing Codelab

00:30:59.590 --> 00:31:00.757
because Minhaz wrote it.

00:31:00.757 --> 00:31:02.590
So let's talk a little
bit about permissions

00:31:02.590 --> 00:31:03.850
because we have private data.

00:31:03.850 --> 00:31:05.350
We have enterprise data.

00:31:05.350 --> 00:31:08.630
There are levels where we
want to secure our data.

00:31:08.630 --> 00:31:10.025
So what can we do about it?

00:31:10.025 --> 00:31:12.400
MINHAZ KAZI: And I believe
Big Query has some permissions

00:31:12.400 --> 00:31:13.260
inside of--

00:31:13.260 --> 00:31:14.176
FELIPE HOFFA: Exactly.

00:31:14.176 --> 00:31:16.480
Big Query has the
Google permissions model

00:31:16.480 --> 00:31:19.800
of who can read, write,
or modify tables.

00:31:19.800 --> 00:31:23.500
And you can make your data
available at that level.

00:31:23.500 --> 00:31:26.540
You can, if you go to the next
screenshot, you can see here,

00:31:26.540 --> 00:31:29.650
like, you can have users by
email, or create Google Groups

00:31:29.650 --> 00:31:32.710
to make easy to manage
them, or with any domain,

00:31:32.710 --> 00:31:35.460
or for all of them to get
the user for public data.

00:31:35.460 --> 00:31:38.470
Or even you can
create views that

00:31:38.470 --> 00:31:41.710
allow people to see your data
but only through a view, which

00:31:41.710 --> 00:31:43.240
is a pretty cool model.

00:31:43.240 --> 00:31:46.360
But you don't even need to
show your data on Big Query

00:31:46.360 --> 00:31:47.610
if you do it on Data Studio.

00:31:47.610 --> 00:31:49.026
MINHAZ KAZI: Yes,
Data Studio also

00:31:49.026 --> 00:31:51.350
has several layers
of permissions.

00:31:51.350 --> 00:31:53.020
If you create a
Data Studio report,

00:31:53.020 --> 00:31:54.970
you can share it with
other people just using

00:31:54.970 --> 00:31:55.960
the drive permissions.

00:31:55.960 --> 00:31:59.030
So you can share it with
a selected group of users.

00:31:59.030 --> 00:32:01.900
You can share it within
your entire organization.

00:32:01.900 --> 00:32:04.780
You can also share it with
everyone who has the URL.

00:32:04.780 --> 00:32:07.150
And you can share it either
for viewing permissions

00:32:07.150 --> 00:32:09.960
or for editing permissions.

00:32:09.960 --> 00:32:11.820
You can also take this
report and embed it.

00:32:11.820 --> 00:32:14.522
So let's say you shared
this with everyone,

00:32:14.522 --> 00:32:15.480
and then you can take--

00:32:15.480 --> 00:32:19.470
that report and embed it in a
Medium post like Felipe did.

00:32:19.470 --> 00:32:21.870
You also get permissions
at the data source level.

00:32:21.870 --> 00:32:24.570
So when you create a data source
and you add the data source

00:32:24.570 --> 00:32:28.080
to a report, you can put in
either viewer's credentials

00:32:28.080 --> 00:32:29.760
or owner's credentials.

00:32:29.760 --> 00:32:32.490
What that means is if
I create the report

00:32:32.490 --> 00:32:35.040
and I put in owner's
credentials and then I

00:32:35.040 --> 00:32:36.960
shared that report
with Felipe and Felipe

00:32:36.960 --> 00:32:40.900
views that dashboard,
Felipe will see my data.

00:32:40.900 --> 00:32:43.230
But if you enable
viewer's credentials,

00:32:43.230 --> 00:32:44.760
when Felipe views
that dashboard,

00:32:44.760 --> 00:32:47.102
Felipe will see Felipe's
data and I will see my data.

00:32:47.102 --> 00:32:48.810
FELIPE HOFFA: And I
can control if people

00:32:48.810 --> 00:32:53.550
want to see my data that I share
with them or if I can only--

00:32:53.550 --> 00:32:56.310
they can only see data they
have the permission to see.

00:32:56.310 --> 00:32:58.950
The permissions get passed all
the way through Data Studio

00:32:58.950 --> 00:33:00.360
into your source.

00:33:00.360 --> 00:33:01.887
MINHAZ KAZI: Yes,
and the good thing

00:33:01.887 --> 00:33:03.970
about community connectors
is that using community

00:33:03.970 --> 00:33:07.020
connectors, you can implement
any kind of permissions model

00:33:07.020 --> 00:33:08.190
that you have.

00:33:08.190 --> 00:33:11.225
If you have your own permissions
and own credential setup,

00:33:11.225 --> 00:33:13.350
you can also flow that
through community connectors

00:33:13.350 --> 00:33:17.010
and add that as a layer
on top of all of this.

00:33:17.010 --> 00:33:18.150
FELIPE HOFFA: Nice.

00:33:18.150 --> 00:33:18.870
MINHAZ KAZI: That
is pretty cool.

00:33:18.870 --> 00:33:20.010
FELIPE HOFFA: That's
how our partners

00:33:20.010 --> 00:33:21.510
create their own connections.

00:33:21.510 --> 00:33:22.590
MINHAZ KAZI: Yes,
most of our partners

00:33:22.590 --> 00:33:24.930
have their own credentials
which will flow through this.

00:33:24.930 --> 00:33:26.190
And then they will--

00:33:26.190 --> 00:33:28.780
they basically know which
user is using the connector.

00:33:28.780 --> 00:33:31.407
And then they can show that
user their specific data.

00:33:31.407 --> 00:33:32.990
We went through all
of these four data

00:33:32.990 --> 00:33:35.450
sets-- the Wikipedia page
views, Stack Overflow trends,

00:33:35.450 --> 00:33:38.490
Google Analytics for Firebase,
and Chrome UX reports.

00:33:38.490 --> 00:33:40.440
That was pretty interesting
for us, I guess.

00:33:40.440 --> 00:33:41.190
FELIPE HOFFA: Yep.

00:33:41.190 --> 00:33:43.310
Hopefully, it was
interesting for you, too.

00:33:43.310 --> 00:33:46.460
Let's go to our takeaways.

00:33:46.460 --> 00:33:48.920
So Big Query is
really, really awesome

00:33:48.920 --> 00:33:51.560
to analyze terabytes of data.

00:33:51.560 --> 00:33:53.510
MINHAZ KAZI: And Data
Studio is more awesome

00:33:53.510 --> 00:33:56.324
because it lets you dashboard
all of your data for free.

00:33:56.324 --> 00:33:57.240
FELIPE HOFFA: Exactly.

00:33:57.240 --> 00:34:00.630
But if you want to
put them together,

00:34:00.630 --> 00:34:02.050
you want some rules in between.

00:34:02.050 --> 00:34:04.133
MINHAZ KAZI: Yes, there
needs to be some something

00:34:04.133 --> 00:34:06.824
in between-- the caching
layer, something in between.

00:34:06.824 --> 00:34:07.740
FELIPE HOFFA: Exactly.

00:34:07.740 --> 00:34:11.750
And there are ways that you can
do this that Materialize will--

00:34:11.750 --> 00:34:14.534
you will have soon and
will be pretty easy to use.

00:34:14.534 --> 00:34:16.159
MINHAZ KAZI: And you
can use Cloud SQL.

00:34:16.159 --> 00:34:17.534
You can use
community connectors.

00:34:17.534 --> 00:34:19.610
Adding a caching
layer will let you

00:34:19.610 --> 00:34:23.319
avoid having, like, exploding
number of queries in Big Query.

00:34:23.319 --> 00:34:24.199
FELIPE HOFFA: Yeah.

00:34:24.199 --> 00:34:25.774
MINHAZ KAZI: So
that is basically

00:34:25.774 --> 00:34:26.940
the end of our presentation.

